    {
      "id": "sticky-00",
      "name": "Template overview",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        0,
        -1400
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 4,
        "width": 640,
        "height": 1060,
        "content": "## Filter and summarise RSS news with AI into Google Sheets\n\n**Who's it for**\nAnyone who has to keep up with a set of news sources - analysts, comms teams, researchers - and\nwants a filtered, summarised digest in a spreadsheet instead of reading every feed by hand.\n\n**How it works**\nSeveral RSS feeds are read in parallel and merged into one list. The first AI agent is a\n**relevance filter**: it judges each headline against your topic and returns a typed verdict with a\nreason, pinned to a schema so the answer is always machine-readable. Only the articles that survive\ngo on to the second stage, which is where the cost is. That agent fetches the full article page\nthrough Browserless - an RSS item usually carries a teaser, not the piece - and writes a structured\nsummary against its own schema. The result is shaped into rows and appended to Google Sheets.\nFiltering before fetching is the whole design: scraping and summarising every item in every feed\nwould cost many times more to produce the same digest.\n\n**How to set up**\n1. Replace the three RSS nodes with your own feed URLs - add or remove as many as you like, they\n   all meet at the same merge.\n2. Attach an OpenRouter credential to both model nodes and a Browserless credential to the fetch node.\n3. Edit the prompt on **`Filter the news`** to describe the topic you actually care about.\n4. Attach Google Sheets and point the last node at your sheet.\n\n**Requirements**\nAn OpenRouter API key, a Browserless account for page fetching, and a Google Sheet.\n\n**How to customize**\nThe relevance test is the prompt on **`Filter the news`**; the digest format is the prompt on\n**`summarise each article`** plus its schema. Change each prompt together with its schema, never\none without the other."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "sticky-01",
      "name": "1. Read every feed",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        280,
        -286
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 480,
        "height": 246,
        "content": "### 1. Read every feed at once\n\nThe three RSS nodes run in parallel and meet at **`combine the feeds`**.\n\nAdd a fourth source by copying any RSS node and wiring it into the same merge - nothing downstream needs to change."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "sticky-02",
      "name": "2. Filter before you spend",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        840,
        340
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 3,
        "width": 480,
        "height": 312,
        "content": "### 2. Filter before you spend\n\n**`Filter the news`** judges each headline for relevance and returns a typed verdict, pinned by **`relevance schema`** below it.\n\nThis runs **before** any page is fetched or summarised, and that ordering is the whole design. Scraping and summarising every item in every feed would cost many times more to produce the same digest."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "sticky-03",
      "name": "3. Fetch, then summarise",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1400,
        -286
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 760,
        "height": 246,
        "content": "### 3. Fetch the page, then summarise\n\nAn RSS item usually carries a teaser, not the article. **`fetch the full article page`** pulls the real page through Browserless so the summary is written from the whole piece.\n\n**`summarise each article`** has its own schema, so every row reaches the sheet with the same fields in the same shape."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    }
